opRoadT -- THE UNKNOWN-OPERATOR REFUSAL, and its negative control.

A token registered in Operators with NO operateMethod used to fall through every arm of
runOP's dispatch chain and return null: the statement parsed, changed nothing, and said
nothing. Four measured casualties before the gate landed on 2026-09-11 -- `eq` (never
registered, answered truthy 3 of 3), `&&` (known token, no road, silent), `AND` (retired
operator, still answers and answers WRONG), and `+/` (banked silent at length 0 the day
before it gained a road). Ten tokens were in that state: | ^ ? >> << :> :< :- & +/

ROAD-A IS THE ANTI-VACUITY SIBLING AND IS NOT DECORATION. A registered operator in the
identical shape must still move the length, or ROAD-B's refusal is just as consistent with
a broken fixture as with a working gate. It wants 1, which a slot that merely defaults to
zero cannot produce.

ROAD-B is the marker that the subject was REACHED. The refusal line below it is asserted
BY THE TOKEN NAME, not by the presence of any refusal -- a gate that refused everything
would satisfy a bare "something was refused" check and would be catastrophic.

ROAD-C must NEVER appear. A refusal is terminal for the unit, so the line after the
refused statement does not run; if it ever prints, the terminal property has been lost and
the refusal has become a warning.

`<<` IS THE STANDING NEGATIVE CONTROL AND MUST STAY METHODLESS. When `+/` gains its
add-member road in the store-operator fold it stops refusing, by design; `<<` keeps this
row honest afterwards. If someone ever gives `<<` an operateMethod, this fixture must be
re-pointed at another methodless token and the re-pin wants a sentence, not a green diff.
